{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Anti-Eviction Act criminal-conviction eviction grounds","requirement":"Landlord may evict a tenant convicted of, or civilly liable for, assault, terrorist threats, theft, drugs or human trafficking, or who knowingly houses such a person; suit must be filed within two years after a three-day Notice to Quit.","key_value":"3-day Notice to Quit; eviction suit within 2 years","coverage_conditions":"Residential tenancies under the Eviction Law; covers eviction where the tenant, or a person the tenant knowingly permits to reside there, was convicted of or civilly found liable for the listed offenses.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Residential rental tenancies under the Eviction Law"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"The landlord may file for eviction, if the tenant has been convicted of or pleaded guilty to, or if a juvenile has been found by the court to be delinquent due to an offense involving assault or terrorist threats against the landlord, a member of the landl ord’s family or an employee of the landlord.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Eviction from owner-occupied two- and three-family dwellings","requirement":"A landlord-occupant of a two- or three-family dwelling may evict only for the listed causes (holding over, nonpayment, disorderly conduct, damage, rule or lease violations) and must give three months' notice for at-will or year-to-year tenancies and one month's notice for month-to-month.","key_value":"3 months' notice; 1 month for month-to-month","coverage_conditions":"Tenants of landlord-occupied two- and three-family dwellings; removal only when a court issues an order for eviction.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":true,"other":"Landlord-occupied two- and three-family dwellings"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.2","quoted_span":"Tenants of landlord-occupied two- and three-family dwellings can be removed only when a court issues an order for eviction. However, in these cases, the landlord must prove that the tenant (a) is staying after the expiration of the term of the lease, (b) is staying after a failure to pay rent,","interaction":null,"confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Rooming and Boarding House eviction good-cause rules","requirement":"Owners of rooming and boarding houses must follow the good-cause and notice requirements of the Eviction Law when evicting residents and must give the County Welfare Board at least three working days' notice before starting an eviction action.","key_value":"3 working days' notice to County Welfare Board","coverage_conditions":"Buildings with at least two living units occupied by unrelated persons without private kitchens and bathrooms that are not exempt under the Rooming and Boarding House Act.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":2,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Rooming and boarding houses"},"exemptions":"Hotels with more than 85 percent temporary occupancy by people with homes elsewhere, school and college dormitories, buildings housing only college students and certain residences for the disabled.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.A.C. 5:27-3.3","quoted_span":"The Regulations Governing Rooming and Boarding Houses, which are enforced by the Bureau of Rooming and Boarding House Standards of the Department of Community Affairs, require owners of rooming and boarding houses to follow the good causes and notice requirements of the Eviction Law when evicting residents, except if otherwise ordered by the Bureau (N.J.A.C. 5:27-3.3(c)).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Penalties for misuse of eviction notices (owner occupancy and removal from residential use)","requirement":"A landlord who evicts to personally occupy the unit must occupy it at least six months; a landlord who boards up, demolishes or retires a building from residential use may not use it residentially for five years.","key_value":"6 months' owner occupancy; 5-year residential-use bar; civil penalty up to $10,000 per violation","coverage_conditions":"Tenants who vacate after a notice of owner occupancy, or after notice that the building will be boarded up, demolished or permanently retired from residential use.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Dwelling units vacated after an eviction notice for owner occupancy or removal from residential use"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"Three times the damages suffered plus attorney fees and costs; civil penalty up to $10,000 for each violation; five-year bar on residential use and on registration as a planned real estate development.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.6","quoted_span":"If the landlord allows any residential use of the unit during the five (5) year period from the date the unit became vacant, the landlord, or the former landlord, may be liable to the tenant for three (3) times the damages plus attorney fees and costs.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Anti-reprisal protection for tenants exercising civil rights","requirement":"A landlord may not retaliate by eviction, substantial lease alteration or refusal to renew against a tenant who enforces lease or legal rights, complains to a government authority, joins a tenant organization or refuses a retaliatory lease change.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All rental properties used for dwelling purposes, including mobile homes, except owner-occupied two- or three-family dwellings.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":true,"other":"All residential rentals other than owner-occupied two- and three-family dwellings"},"exemptions":"Owner-occupied two- and three-family dwellings.","penalty":"The tenant may sue the landlord for damages in a civil action.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:42-10.10","quoted_span":"A landlord cannot take reprisal action against a tenant by eviction, substantial alteration of a lease or its terms, or refusal to renew a lease when a tenant exercises certain civil rights (N.J.S.A. 2A:42-10.10).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Foreclosure eviction protections for residential tenants","requirement":"Foreclosure alone is not grounds for eviction; a foreclosure buyer who wants to occupy the unit as a primary residence must give the tenant a 90-day notice to quit, and any lease extending beyond 90 days must be honored.","key_value":"90-day notice to quit; 10 business days to notify tenants of sale","coverage_conditions":"Residential tenants in foreclosed properties, including month-to-month tenants and two- and three-family owner-occupied units.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Residential rental units in foreclosed properties"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:50-69","quoted_span":"Before a tenant can be evicted due to foreclosure, the landlord must provide the tenant with a 90 -day notice to qui t when the foreclosed property has been purchased by a buyer who wants to personally occupy it as his or her primary residence.","interaction":"Federal law does not preempt State or local law that provides longer time periods or other additional protections for tenants in foreclosure proceedings.","confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Senior Citizens and Disabled Protected Tenancy Act","requirement":"Tenants aged 62 or older before the conversion recording, permanently disabled tenants, or certain disabled veterans who lived in the building at least one year before recording are protected from eviction for up to 40 years if family income is at or below the cap.","key_value":"40 years' protection; income limit 3 times county per-person average or $50,000, whichever is greater","coverage_conditions":"Tenants in buildings converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership who meet the age, disability, veteran and one-year residency tests.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Buildings converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.22 to -61.39","quoted_span":"Tenants may be protected from eviction for 40 years if their family income is not more than three (3) times the average per person income in their county or $50,000.00, whichever is greater (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.28).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Tenant Protection Act of 1992 (Hudson County conversion protections)","requirement":"The Act extends protected-tenancy protections to qualified tenants in buildings being converted in qualified counties who were not eligible as senior citizens or disabled persons under the 1981 Act; only Hudson County currently qualifies.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Qualified tenants in buildings converted or being converted in qualified counties; at present the only qualified county is Hudson County.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Buildings converted or being converted in qualified counties (Hudson County)"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.40 to -61.59","quoted_span":"The Tenant Protection Law of 1992 amendment extends protections to qualified tenants in qualified counties in buildings converted or being converted who were not eligible for Protected Tenancy as either Senior Citizens or Disabled Persons under the “Senior Citizens and Disabled Protected Tenancy Act of 1981” (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.40 to -61.59; N.J.A.C. 5:24-3.1 to -3.4).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.65,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Prohibition on rent increases above rent control or unconscionable increases","requirement":"A landlord may not raise rent above the amount allowed by a rent control or rent leveling ordinance; where no rent control applies, an unreasonable or unconscionable rent increase is prohibited.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Rent-controlled residential properties and properties not subject to rent control.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Residential rental properties, rent-controlled and not rent-controlled"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":null,"quoted_span":"An increase in rent in excess of any rent control or rent leveling ordinance, or if the property is not subject to rent control, an unreasonable or unconscionable rent increase.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.4,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
